Think of melt() as turning columns into rows.

In real-world data, you may receive data in a wide format, where each month, product, department, or metric is stored as a separate column. For analysis, you often need long format, where those column names become values in a single column.

Real-time example: Monthly Sales Report

Suppose a company gives you this sales report:

| Employee | Department |   Jan |   Feb |   Mar |
| -------- | ---------- | ----: | ----: | ----: |
| Ravi     | Sales      | 50000 | 55000 | 60000 |
| Priya    | Sales      | 45000 | 52000 | 58000 |
| Arun     | Marketing  | 30000 | 35000 | 40000 |


This is wide format.

You want to analyze sales month-by-month. Instead of having Jan, Feb, and Mar as separate columns, you want:

| Employee | Department | Month | Sales |
| -------- | ---------- | ----- | ----: |
| Ravi     | Sales      | Jan   | 50000 |
| Ravi     | Sales      | Feb   | 55000 |
| Ravi     | Sales      | Mar   | 60000 |
| Priya    | Sales      | Jan   | 45000 |
| Priya    | Sales      | Feb   | 52000 |
| Priya    | Sales      | Mar   | 58000 |
| Arun     | Marketing  | Jan   | 30000 |
| Arun     | Marketing  | Feb   | 35000 |
| Arun     | Marketing  | Mar   | 40000 |

This is long format.

melt() does exactly this transformation.

======

pd.melt() is a pandas function used to transform a DataFrame from wide format 
to long format by converting selected columns into rows while keeping identifier columns unchanged.

